---
import BlogPostLayout from '../../../../layouts/BlogPostLayout.astro';

const title = 'Highest package vs median CTC: what they mean in college placements';
const description =
  'Highest package is one student\'s best offer; median CTC is the middle salary of the batch. See what each means in placements, with a worked example.';
const canonical =
  'https://futurecareerschool.com/blog/college-degrees/highest-package-vs-median-ctc-meaning-india/';
const publishedDateISO = '2026-11-15';
const publishedDateLabel = '15 November 2026';
const authorName = 'Shivanshi Sehgal';
const authorUrl = 'https://futurecareerschool.com/about/shivanshi-sehgal/';
const authorRole = 'Career researcher and writer';

const jumpLinks = [
  { id: 'median-ctc', label: 'What is median CTC?' },
  { id: 'highest-package', label: 'Highest package, average and median side by side' },
  { id: 'real-numbers', label: 'Real numbers: IIT Bombay, 2024-25' },
  { id: 'placement-meaning', label: 'What does placement mean in college?' },
  { id: 'ctc-not-salary', label: 'Why CTC is not your monthly pay' },
  { id: 'which-number', label: 'Which number to use for which decision' },
  { id: 'ask-placement-cell', label: 'Three questions to ask the placement cell' },
  { id: 'above-median', label: 'How to land above the median' },
  { id: 'faq', label: 'FAQs' },
];

const batchRows = [
  { student: 'Students 1 to 2', pay: '4 and 4', note: 'The lowest offers' },
  { student: 'Students 3 to 4', pay: '4.5 and 5', note: 'Below the middle' },
  { student: 'Student 5', pay: '5', note: 'The middle student: this is the median' },
  { student: 'Students 6 to 8', pay: '5.5, 6 and 7', note: 'Above the middle' },
  { student: 'Student 9', pay: '48', note: 'One rare offer: this is the highest package' },
];

const numberCards = [
  {
    tone: 'gold',
    label: 'Highest package',
    answers: 'What is the best offer any one student got?',
    watch: 'It can belong to one or two students out of hundreds, and it may include a joining bonus or other one-time pay.',
  },
  {
    tone: 'violet',
    label: 'Average package',
    answers: 'If every salary were shared equally, what would each student get?',
    watch: 'A few very large offers pull the average up, so most students earn less than it.',
  },
  {
    tone: 'teal',
    label: 'Median CTC',
    answers: 'What did the student in the middle of the batch get?',
    watch: 'It hides the spread. Ask for the lowest offers and the branch-wise median too.',
  },
];

const iitRows = [
  { year: '2023-24', average: '23.5', median: '17.92' },
  { year: '2024-25', average: '26.45', median: '20' },
];

const placementSteps = [
  {
    title: 'A company offers a job through the college',
    body: 'The company visits the campus or runs an online drive for the college. A student who clears the tests and interviews gets an offer.',
  },
  {
    title: 'The college counts that student as placed',
    body: 'Most colleges count an accepted offer. Some also count internships or stipend-only roles, so ask what counts.',
  },
  {
    title: 'The placement percentage divides placed students by a group',
    body: 'The group can be the whole batch, the students eligible for placements, or only the students who registered. The bigger the group, the lower the percentage.',
  },
];

const ctcRows = [
  {
    part: 'Fixed pay',
    paid: 'Yes, every month, before deductions',
    note: 'The most dependable part. Two offers with the same CTC can have very different fixed pay.',
  },
  {
    part: 'Variable or performance bonus',
    paid: 'Only if targets are met',
    note: 'Some employers count it in CTC at the target amount, so you may receive less.',
  },
  {
    part: 'Joining bonus',
    paid: 'Usually paid once',
    note: 'It lifts the first-year CTC above what you earn in later years.',
  },
  {
    part: 'Employer PF, gratuity and insurance',
    paid: 'Not in your bank account',
    note: 'The employer spends this on you, but it is not monthly cash.',
  },
];

const decisionRows = [
  {
    title: 'Comparing two colleges',
    body: 'Compare the median CTC of your own branch for the last three years, side by side, and ignore the highest package. A college with a lower headline and a steadier median is often the safer bet.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Checking whether a fee is worth it',
    body: 'Set the median fixed pay, after tax and PF, against the full cost of the course. If you need a loan, a median that leaves little room after repayments is a warning, whatever the highest package says. This is general information, not financial advice; get any loan terms in writing.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Reading a brochure headline',
    body: 'Treat the highest package as proof that some recruiters pay well, nothing more. If the median is missing next to it, ask for it before you believe the page.',
  },
];

const askItems = [
  {
    title: 'What is the median CTC for my branch in the last three years?',
    body: 'A median across all branches hides weak ones. Ask for it by branch and by year, and ask whether it covers only placed students.',
  },
  {
    title: 'How much of the CTC is fixed pay?',
    body: 'Ask for the median fixed pay, or a sample offer letter with the breakup. This is the number that decides your monthly income.',
  },
  {
    title: 'How many placed students got offers below the median?',
    body: 'The spread tells you more than one number. If the lowest offers are far below the median, plan for that case too.',
  },
];

const faqItems = [
  {
    q: 'What do highest package vs median CTC mean in placements?',
    a: 'The highest package is the single biggest offer any student in the batch received. The median CTC is the offer in the middle, where half the placed students got more and half got less. The highest shows the top of what recruiters paid; the median shows what a typical student got.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What does median CTC mean?',
    a: 'Median CTC is the middle salary when every placed student\'s package is lined up from lowest to highest. CTC stands for cost to company, the yearly amount the employer spends on you, so it includes more than the cash you receive each month.',
  },
  {
    q: 'In college, what does highest package or placement mean, in simple words?',
    a: 'Placement means a company hired you through your college. The highest package is the biggest salary offered to any one student, and the placement percentage is how many students got a job offer out of a chosen group. Always ask which group the percentage uses.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is median CTC the same as average package?',
    a: 'No. The average adds all salaries and divides by the number of students, so a few huge offers push it up. The median is not moved by those offers, which is why it is usually closer to what most students got.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is the median CTC what I will earn?',
    a: 'Not exactly. It describes a past batch, not you, and your offer depends on your branch, skills, interview performance and the year\'s hiring. CTC is also not monthly pay, so check the fixed pay and the in-hand amount.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why did the IITs stop publishing the highest package?',
    a: 'For the 2024-25 season the All IITs Placement Committee decided not to disclose the highest package and to publish only median and average pay. Placement officials said very large offers were raising expectations and adding pressure on students.',
  },
];

const faqJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqItems.map((item) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: item.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: item.a },
  })),
};
---

<BlogPostLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  category="college-degrees"
  publishedAt={publishedDateISO}
  publishedLabel={publishedDateLabel}
  authorName={authorName}
  authorUrl={authorUrl}
  authorRole={authorRole}
  jsonLdExtras={[faqJsonLd]}
>
  <p class="lead-para"><strong>Highest package is the single biggest salary offered to one student in a batch, and median CTC is the middle salary, where half the placed students earned more and half earned less.</strong>
    If you are comparing colleges, trust the median, because it shows what a typical student got.</p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Highest package</strong> is one student's best offer. <strong>Median CTC</strong> is the middle student's offer. The average adds everything up, so a few huge offers pull it above what most students got.</li>
      <li>Plan with the median. It is the nearest thing to a typical outcome, and it is the number to test a fee or a loan against.</li>
      <li>CTC is what the employer spends in a year, not your monthly pay. The skills and proof of work you build on top of the college are what move you above its median, and they are part of the decisions that unlock high income opportunities and earlier financial freedom.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p>If a college fee or a loan depends on these numbers, <a href="/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/career-counselling-for-college-students/">career counselling for college students</a> can help you test the choice before you pay. More guides on this topic sit in <a href="/blog/college-degrees/">College and Degrees</a>.</p>

  <nav class="jump-nav" aria-label="Article sections">
    <p class="jump-nav__label">Jump to section</p>
    <ul class="jump-nav__list">
      {jumpLinks.map((item) => (
        <li><a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.label}</a></li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="median-ctc">What is median CTC? The middle salary, in plain words</h2>

  <p>Line up every placed student from the lowest salary to the highest. The student standing in the exact middle has the median CTC. LPA means lakh per annum, and one lakh is 1,00,000 rupees.</p>

  <p>Here is a small batch of nine placed students. It is an illustration, not a real college.</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Who</th>
          <th>Offer in LPA</th>
          <th>What it shows</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {batchRows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Who"><strong>{row.student}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Offer in LPA">{row.pay}</td>
            <td data-label="What it shows">{row.note}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>The highest package is 48 LPA. The average is about 9.9 LPA, because the one big offer drags it up.</p>

  <p>The median CTC is 5 LPA. Eight of the nine students got 7 LPA or less, so the median describes this batch far better than the other two numbers.</p>

  <p>With an even number of students, there is no single middle student. Take the two middle salaries and find their halfway point.</p>

  <h2 id="highest-package">Highest package, average and median: what each one answers</h2>

  <p>These three numbers can come from the same batch and tell three different stories. Each one answers a different question.</p>

  <div class="number-grid">
    {numberCards.map((card) => (
      <div class={`number-card number-card--${card.tone}`}>
        <p class="number-card__label">{card.label}</p>
        <p class="number-card__row number-card__row--fit"><strong>Answers:</strong> {card.answers}</p>
        <p class="number-card__row number-card__row--warn"><strong>Watch out:</strong> {card.watch}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <p>A college that shows only the highest package is showing you its best day. A college that shows the median, the average and the lowest offers is showing you the batch.</p>

  <h2 id="real-numbers">Real numbers: IIT Bombay, 2024-25</h2>

  <p>IIT Bombay's 2024-25 placement season shows the gap between average and median in a real batch. <a href="https://www.freepressjournal.in/education/iit-bombay-average-salary-hits-rs-2645-lakh-1422-students-placed" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Free Press Journal reported</a> that 2,469 students registered, 2,040 took part in recruitment and 1,422 students were placed.</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Season</th>
          <th>Average CTC in LPA</th>
          <th>Median CTC in LPA</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {iitRows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Season"><strong>{row.year}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Average CTC in LPA">{row.average}</td>
            <td data-label="Median CTC in LPA">{row.median}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>In 2024-25 the average was 6.45 LPA above the median. A few very large offers lifted the average, and the middle student got less than it.</p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Why the highest package is missing</p>
    <p>For 2024-25, <a href="https://news.careers360.com/iit-placements-2024-2025-no-highest-package-data-bombay-delhi-patna-roorkee-crore-salary-btech-jobs-median-average-lowest-aipc" target="_blank" rel="noopener">the All IITs Placement Committee decided not to disclose the highest package</a> and to publish only median and average salaries. Officials said crore-plus headlines were setting expectations very high and adding pressure on students.</p>
    <p>Rankings use the median too. The <a href="https://nirfindia.org/nirfpdfcdn/2024/framework/Management.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NIRF management framework</a> scores the median salary of graduates over the previous three years, not the highest.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="placement-meaning">What does placement mean in college, in simple words?</h2>

  <p>Placement means a company hires you through your college. Students often hear "highest package" and "placement percentage" together, so it helps to see how the two numbers are made.</p>

  <ol class="rich-list">
    {placementSteps.map((step) => (
      <li><strong>{step.title}.</strong> {step.body}</li>
    ))}
  </ol>

  <div class="highlight-box">
    <p><strong>An illustration, not a real college.</strong> A batch has 120 students. Eighty register for placements and 72 get offers.</p>
    <p>The college can say 90% placed (72 of 80 registered) or 60% placed (72 of 120 students). Both are true, and only one of them describes your odds.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="ctc-not-salary">Why CTC is not your monthly pay</h2>

  <p>CTC means cost to company: the total the employer spends on you in a year. It is not the amount that reaches your bank account.</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Part of the offer</th>
          <th>Do you get it as cash?</th>
          <th>Why it matters</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {ctcRows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Part of the offer"><strong>{row.part}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Do you get it as cash?">{row.paid}</td>
            <td data-label="Why it matters">{row.note}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>On top of this, tax and your own PF contribution are deducted. In <a href="https://cleartax.in/s/how-to-find-your-in-hand-salary-based-on-ctc" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ClearTax's worked example</a>, a Rs 7.5 lakh CTC comes to about Rs 54,283 a month in hand, while Rs 7.5 lakh divided by 12 is Rs 62,500. Exact amounts depend on the employer, the state and your tax position.</p>

  <p>Two offers can share a headline too. In offer letters compiled by <a href="https://www.careers360.com/mba/articles/mba-ctc-vs-in-hand-salary-viral-reddit-offer-letters-expose-what-in-hand-salary-mba-graduates-have" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Careers360</a>, Dabur and Kotak both showed a Rs 15 lakh CTC, but Dabur's fixed pay was Rs 12.5 lakh and Kotak's was Rs 15 lakh.</p>

  <p>The four labour codes also took effect on <a href="https://labour.gov.in/sites/default/files/pib2192463.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">21 November 2025</a>, and employers may now rework how pay is split between basic pay and allowances. Ask for the written breakup before you compare two offers.</p>

  <h2 id="which-number">Which number to use for which decision</h2>

  <div class="decision-stack">
    {decisionRows.map((row, i) => (
      <div class="decision-row">
        <span class="decision-row__num">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
        <div>
          <p class="decision-row__title">{row.title}</p>
          <p class="decision-row__body">{row.body}</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2 id="ask-placement-cell">Three questions to ask the placement cell</h2>

  <p>You do not need a long audit to get a straight answer. These three questions, sent in one message, show whether the college is comfortable with its own numbers.</p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    {askItems.map((item, i) => (
      <div class={`compare-card compare-card--${['teal', 'gold', 'violet'][i]}`}>
        <p class="compare-card__label">Question {i + 1}</p>
        <p class="compare-card__title">{item.title}</p>
        <p class="compare-card__body">{item.body}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2 id="above-median">How to land above the median</h2>

  <p>The median is where a typical student of that college lands. Students who beat it usually have something the median does not measure: a project they can show, a clear way of explaining their work, and a few people who have checked that work.</p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>A high median does not guarantee you a high offer, and a low one does not cap you. Build one visible piece of work in your field, learn to explain it in a couple of minutes, and get a few real people to check it. That is how a high-income skill portfolio starts, and it is the part of the outcome you control.</p>
  </div>

  <p>To check a whole report, not only these two numbers, read <a href="/blog/college-degrees/how-to-read-college-placement-report-india/">how to read a college placement report</a>. To line up several colleges, use <a href="/blog/college-degrees/how-to-compare-colleges-for-career-outcome-india/">how to compare colleges for career outcome</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="faq">FAQs on highest package and median CTC</h2>

  <div class="faq-accordion">
    {faqItems.map((item) => (
      <details class="faq-item" open>
        <summary>{item.q}</summary>
        <div class="faq-a">{item.a}</div>
      </details>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2 id="next-step">Next step</h2>

  <p>Ask your shortlisted colleges for the branch-wise median CTC and fixed pay, then compare them with the full cost of the course. If the campus drive looks thin, <a href="/blog/job-search/campus-vs-off-campus-placement-india/">campus vs off-campus placement</a> shows when going outside it is the smarter route.</p>

